],"best_idea":"When the camera's eye is pinned by a wall, a vertical look drag tilts the view through a one-sided bias while the eye stays put, and the bias eases home at a fixed speed once the wall clears.","decision":"adapt","decision_why":"The donor pulls the eye in on a wall and tilts the view; Frankendom keeps the eye pull-in and takes only the clip-gated, one-sided look bias and the fixed-duration cosine ease, because it gives the shoulder shot a way to look over a wall without swinging into it. Drop the water corridor, head bob and terrain tilt. Its bias is one-sided, so it does not help when looking down; say so in the plan. Numbers must be Frankendom's own, not copied from the WoW binary the comments cite.","decision_status":"proposed","acceptance":"A player with a wall behind a shoulder camera drags the view upward: the eye does not move, the view tilts up over the wall, and when the wall clears the view eases back to level at 90 degrees per second. A client unit test asserts that with clipped set and a vertical drag the eye position is unchanged while the bias rises."}
